.TH ABS 3
.SH NAME
abs \- integer absolute value
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B int abs(int i);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Abs
returns
the absolute value of its integer operand.
.SH SEE ALSO
floor(3) for
.I fabs
.SH BUGS
You get what the hardware gives on the largest negative integer.
.PP
.B Note:
that is still true, and the number is different here.  An
.B int
is a 41-bit two's-complement field, so INT_MIN is \-2**40 and its magnitude,
2**40, has no positive twin in 41 bits.  The same asymmetry is what
.IR strtol (3)
had to be rewritten around and what
.IR printf (3)
of the most negative value gets wrong if the magnitude is taken by negating.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/abs.c
